  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What do the springs on a garage door do?",
      "a": "They balance the door. Twisted torsion springs or stretched springs store energy as the door closes and give it back as it opens, so the door feels almost weightless.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are garage door springs dangerous?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a small motor lift a heavy garage door?",
      "a": "The springs carry nearly all the weight, so the motor only needs to overcome friction and small differences. A well-balanced door can even be lifted by hand.",
      "asks": [
        "How strong is a garage door motor?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a garage door go back up if something is in the way?",
      "a": "A safety beam across the bottom of the doorway is watched by a sensor. If something breaks it while the door is closing, the opener stops and reverses. Many also reverse if they feel too much resistance.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the garage door safety sensor work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the remote open the garage door?",
      "a": "The remote sends a coded radio signal. The opener checks the code, which changes each time on modern systems, and runs the motor if it matches.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a rolling code?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why should you not fix garage door springs yourself?",
      "a": "The springs store a huge amount of energy. If one snaps or is released wrongly, it can whip round and cause serious injury, so trained technicians handle them.",
      "asks": [
        "Can I replace a garage door spring?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Garage door springs counterbalance the door so the opener motor only has to move a nearly weightless load.",
    "Garage door openers use a photo-eye beam near the floor to reverse the door if something blocks it."
